WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.678
DNP3, or Distributed Network Protocol version 3, is widely associated with electric utilities,

00:00:05.678 --> 00:00:08.681
water systems, and other geographically distributed operations.

00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:15.117
It supports telemetry and control across links that may have limited bandwidth or intermittent connectivity,

00:00:15.117 --> 00:00:17.262
including serial and IP-based networks.

00:00:17.262 --> 00:00:20.695
A DNP3 master, sometimes called a controlling station,

00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:23.698
communicates with outstations located near field equipment.

00:00:23.698 --> 00:00:28.847
The master requests data or directs approved operations, while outstations report measurements,

00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:30.134
status, and events.

00:00:30.134 --> 00:00:35.712
This model supports centralized supervision of assets spread across a large physical area.

00:00:35.712 --> 00:00:42.148
DNP3 can report changes as events rather than requiring every value to be transferred continuously.

00:00:42.148 --> 00:00:43.864
Event classes, time information,

00:00:43.864 --> 00:00:49.013
and unsolicited reporting can help a control center receive important changes efficiently.

00:00:49.013 --> 00:00:55.449
These features also give defenders useful behavioral context when collection and decoding are configured correctly.

00:00:55.449 --> 00:00:59.740
As with other industrial protocols, deployment age and configuration vary.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.888
Traditional DNP3 did not assume the same hostile network conditions expected today.

00:01:04.888 --> 00:01:09.608
DNP3 Secure Authentication can provide message-authentication protections for selected critical operations,

00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:14.328
but it is not a substitute for complete architecture, access control,

00:01:14.328 --> 00:01:15.615
or confidentiality planning.

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:19.905
Defenders should understand expected master and outstation relationships, communication paths,

00:01:19.905 --> 00:01:22.051
event rates, and operating windows.

00:01:22.051 --> 00:01:25.483
New masters, unusual control operations, unexplained communication loss,

00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:30.632
or changes in reporting behavior deserve investigation with utility and engineering personnel.

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:34.064
Protection combines segmentation, controlled remote and engineering access,

00:01:34.064 --> 00:01:37.926
secure configuration, monitoring, time synchronization, and tested recovery procedures.

00:01:37.926 --> 00:01:40.929
DNP3 knowledge helps analysts describe what occurred,

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:45.220
but process owners determine operational consequence and the safest response.
